# TackleNet usage notebook

TackleNet contains 737 American football practice clips with six Standard
Assessment of Tackling Technique (SATT) scores and a zero-based first point
of contact (FPOC) frame index. This notebook checks the annotations and
frozen split, plots dataset statistics, and demonstrates the 32-frame
contact window on locally available videos.

SATT3 grades 0–1 map to risky technique and grades 2–3 map to safe technique.
The sum of the six SATT scores is descriptive. The binary label comes from
SATT3.

Data are available under CC BY 4.0. Benchmark code is released under the MIT
License.
## 1. Local setup

Install the packages in `requirements.txt` from the repository or review
folder, then open the notebook in Jupyter and select **Run All**. A standalone
environment needs NumPy, pandas, Matplotlib, scikit-learn, OpenCV, and
Jupyter. The notebook reads local files after setup.

- **Extracted review or dataset folder.** Keep
  `annotations_satt_fpoc.csv` and `videos/` together. An optional
  `splits_frozen.csv` can sit beside the annotation file.
- **Repository.** Open from the root or `notebooks/`. The notebook reads
  the CSVs in `splits/` and looks for clips in `videos/` or
  `tacklenet_data/videos/`. With only the CSVs it runs the annotation
  and split sections.
- **Separate video subset.** Set `VIDEO_DIR` to its video folder. The
  MP4 filenames must match the full annotation CSV. Set `DATA_ROOT`
  if the annotation files are in a different folder from the notebook.
